 "voice": [
  {
   "id": "vo01",
   "t": 0.1,
   "dur": 4.688,
   "file": "vo/01.wav",
   "text": "Where does your breath go? Not into a bag. Into these."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo02",
   "t": 5.5,
   "dur": 10.349,
   "file": "vo/02.wav",
   "text": "Below your windpipe, the airways fork again and again, about twenty-three times. At the tips hang tiny air pockets, half a billion of them, each a fifth of a millimetre."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo03",
   "t": 16.7,
   "dur": 10.484,
   "file": "vo/03.wav",
   "text": "For centuries, physicians taught the lung was clotted blood, there to cool the heart. In sixteen sixty-one, Marcello Malpighi put an animal's lung under a microscope: bubbles."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo04",
   "t": 27.9,
   "dur": 12.685,
   "file": "vo/04.wav",
   "text": "Each pocket is wrapped in tiny blood vessels. In places, the wall between air and blood is far thinner than a red cell. Oxygen just slips across, no pump needed. Thicken that wall, and breathing gets harder."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo05",
   "t": 43.1,
   "dur": 8.407,
   "file": "vo/05.wav",
   "text": "Spread every pocket out flat, and they'd cover about a quarter of a tennis court. All of that, folded up inside your chest."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo06",
   "t": 55.1,
   "dur": 4.497,
   "file": "vo/06.wav",
   "text": "So your lungs aren't a bag. They're a sponge that spreads air thin."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo07",
   "t": 60.3,
   "dur": 5.725,
   "file": "vo/07.wav",
   "text": "Meanwhile, as you watched, air went in and out of you. About six litres of it."
  },
  {
   "id": "vo08",
   "t": 67.1,
   "dur": 4.355,
   "file": "vo/08.wav",
   "text": "Next: a cell that leaves your blood. It squeezes through a wall."
  }
 ]
}